## KEYWORDS
perovskite solar cells, vanadium oxide, hole transport layer, band alignment, hysteresis
## ABSTRACT
An inverted planar heterojunction perovskite solar cell (PSC) is one of the most competitive photovoltaic devices exhibiting a high power conversion efficiency (PCE) and nearly free hysteresis in the voltage–current output. However, the band alignment between the transport materials and the perovskite absorber has not been optimized, resulting in a lower open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ than that of regular PSCs. To address this issue, we tune the band alignment in perovskite photovoltaic architecture by introducing bilayer structured transport materials, e.g., the hole transport material poly(ethylenedioxythiophene):poly(styrene sulfonate) $\mathrm{(PEDOT:PSS)/V_{2}O_{5}}.$ . In this study, solution processed inorganic $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer is incorporated into PEDOT:PSS for achieving improved film surface properties as well as optical and electrical properties. For example, the work function (WF) was changed from 5.1 to $5.4\mathrm{eV.}$ A remarkably high PCE of $17.5\%$ with nearly free hysteresis and a stabilized efficiency of $17.1\%$ have been achieved. Electronic impedance spectra (EIS) demonstrate a significant increase in the recombination resistance after introducing the interlayer, associated with the high $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ output value of $1.05\mathrm{V}.$ Transient photocurrent and photovoltage measurements indicate that a comparable charge transport process and an inhibited recombination process occur in the PSC with the introduction of the $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer.
## 1 Introduction
Organic inorganic halide perovskite-based (e.g., $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{X}}_{3},$ $\qquadX=\mathbf{Cl},$ , Br, I) solar cells (PSCs) have attracted considerable attention and achieved impressive progresses owing to their outstanding photo-electronic properties, including high extinction coefficient $(\sim10^{4}-$ $10^{5}\mathrm{cm}^{-1},$ ) [1, 2], low exciton binding energy $({\sim}30\mathrm{meV})$ [3], and long carrier diffusion lengths in thin films $(>1\upmu\mathrm{m})$ [4, 5]. Since the breakthrough of replacing the liquid hole-transport electrolyte with the first solid hole transport material, $2{,}2{^{\prime}}{,}7{,}7{^{\prime}}$ -tetrakis-(N,N-di-pmethoxyphenlamine)- ${\cdot9,9^{\prime}}$ -spiro-bifluorene (spiroOMeTAD), in PSCs for achieving a power conversion efficiency (PCE) higher than $9\%$ [6], remarkable efforts have been made to improve the PCE to ${>}20\%$ [7–10]. High-performance PSCs in both mesoporous scaffolds and planar heterojunction architectures have been demonstrated. Among different types of device architectures, a simplified planar heterojunction structured device, particularly with an inverted p–i–n structure, e.g., poly(ethylenedioxythiophene): poly(styrene sulfonate) (PEDOT:PSS) $\mathrm{\langleCH_{3}N H_{3}P b{X_{3}}/}$ phenyl- $C_{61}$ -butyric acid methyl ester (PCBM), has gradually emerged as one of the most promising technologies. This could be due to its low temperature fabrication method and the excellent steady-state output obtained in the device characteristics, originating from significantly reduced hysteresis [11]. However, the inverted planar heterojunction structure PSC exhibits imperfect band alignment, leading to a relatively inferior device PCE, when compared to the state of art device performance based on regular PSCs [10].
In inverted planar heterojunction structure PSCs, $C_{60}$ or PCBM is commonly used as the electron transport layer (ETL). PEDOT:PSS is commonly employed as the hole transport layer (HTL), which has been widely applied in organic solar cells owing to the simplicity of its processing, high conductivity, and good film processibility [12]. Since PSCs based on inverted planar structure were successfully demonstrated by Guo et al. [13] for the first time, their device performance has been enhanced from ${\sim}4\%$ to ${>}18\%$ [14] by developing film fabrication techniques for perovskite materials and interface engineering of the devices [15–21]. However, one of the biggest drawbacks of p–i–n structure is that the device performs at a relatively low open circuit voltage (normally $<1\mathrm{V}$ ) than that of the conventional $\mathsf{n{-}i{-}p}$ structure (normally $\mathrm{~>~}1\mathrm{~V~}$ ). Furthermore, the imperfect band alignment between perovskite and the commonly used HTL PEDOT:PSS [22] is also an issue. The work function (WF) of PEDOT:PSS is ${\sim}4.9{\mathrm{-}}5.1\mathrm{eV},$ , which is lower than that of the valence band of the perovskite layer $(5.4\mathrm{eV})$ , leading to an energy loss in the carrier transport from perovskite to ${\mathrm{HTL}},$ causing an open-circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ loss. To address this issue, PEDOT:PSS was modified with different interfacial materials or replaced with other HTL having a high WF. The common interfacial materials used are organic polymers, such as poly(N,N’-bis(4-butylphenyl)-N,N’- bis(phenyl)benzidine) (poly-TPD), (N-90-heptadecanyl2,7-carbazole-alt-5,5-(40,70-di-2-thienyl-20,10,30-benzothiadiazole) (PCDTBT), and poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6- trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA), with deep highest occupied molecule orbital (HOMO) levels $(5.3\mathrm{-}5.5\mathrm{eV})$ [19–21], which were used to modify the PEDOT:PSS surface. The devices based on the bilayer HTL (PEDOT:PSS/polymer) exhibited an enhanced $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ $(>1\mathrm{V})$ . However, high vacuum based evaporation processes are required for preparing perovskite films that form a good contact with the HTL since these polymers are usually hydrophobic in nature, which effectively increases the complexity and cost of fabrication. Alternatively, inorganic p-type semiconductor materials, including copper compounds (CuSCN, CuI, ${\mathrm{CuO}}_{x},$ etc.) and transition metal oxides (TMOs) such as NiO, $\mathrm{MoO}_{3},$ , ${\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{5},$ and $\mathsf{W O}_{3}$ exhibiting relatively high WFs can be used as excellent candidates for modifying or replacing PEDOT:PSS and enable an enlarged quasi-Fermi energy splitting in the photovoltaics device. Electrodeposited CuSCN thin film $(\sim70\mathrm{nm})$ was used as an ${\mathrm{HTL}},$ resulting in a PCE of $16.6\%$ for the fabricated PSC [23]. When CuI was employed to replace PEDOT:PSS, a PCE of $13.58\%$ could be achieved with a $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of up to $1.04\mathrm{V}$ [24]. Solution processed $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}$ and $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -doped $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}$ were later employed as the ${\mathrm{HTL}},$ resulting in PCEs of $8\%$ and $15.4\%$ , respectively, with the open circuit voltages reaching as high as $1.1\mathrm{V}$ [25]. Recently, Seok et al. fabricated an inverted structure using $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}$ film deposited via pulsed laser deposition (PLD) to achieve a device efficiency exceeding $17\%$ [26]. $\mathrm{MoO}_{3}$ was employed to modify the PEDOT:PSS/indium tin oxide (ITO) interface in order to improve the PCE and stability of the PSC [27]. However, the fabrication of most effective inorganic HTLs requires a relatively high temperature or high vacuum processes, which decreases the processing compatibility of the
photovoltaic devices.
Herein, we use inorganic vanadium oxide $(\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x})$ as an efficient interfacial material in perovskite heterojunctions for achieving improved band alignment and device performances. Solution-processed $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ is incorporated in the PEDOT:PSS/perovskite interface, and a bilayer structure consisting $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ and PEDOT:PSS acts as the HTL in the inverted PSCs. X-ray photoelectron spectra (XPS), ultra violet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS), UVvis and tapping-mode atomic force microscopy (AFM) measurements are employed to explore the compositional, electronic, optical, and surface properties of the bilayer HTL. The introduction of $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ in the HTL causes a change in the WF from 5.1 to ${\sim}5.4\mathrm{eV},$ without negatively affecting the transmittance properties and the surface roughness. A PCE of $17.5\%$ with nearly free hysteresis, featuring a high $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.05\mathrm{V},$ has been achieved. Electronic impedance spectra (EIS) analysis of the devices demonstrate that the recombination resistance is increased by nearly one order of magnitude at high bias voltages after the modification, implying an improved $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ . Transient measurements (transient photocurrent and transient photovoltage) reveal that a comparable charge transport process and an inhibited charge recombination process occur in bilayer HTLbased devices as compared with the devices based on pristine PEDOT:PSS. The currently used inorganic material, ${\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{x},$ and other hole transport materials (e.g., $\mathrm{NiO}_{x}\mathrm{,}$ ), which can be processed at low temperatures, exhibit a great potential in the fabrication of other related semiconductors and optoelectronics for improving the device performances.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Vanadium oxides synthesis
Vanadium oxide precursor was prepared according to a previous report [28]. Metal powders were purchased from Aladdin Reagent. $0.1\mathrm{g}$ of vanadium metal powder was dispersed in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol under stirring for several minutes. Then $1.0\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $(30\%)$ ) solution was added to the metal powder suspension. After ${12\mathrm{h}},$ the color of the vanadium oxide solution turned to orange and subsequently to brown. The solution was dried in a vacuum chamber, and the dried vanadium oxide was then dispersed uniformly in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ ethanol. After filtering with a $0.2\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{m}$ filter, a solution having a concentration of ${\sim}10~\mathrm{mg{\cdot}m L^{-1}}$ was prepared. Thus, the hydrogen vanadium bronze $\mathrm{(H}_{\boldsymbol{x}}\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{5})$ solution was prepared.
## 2.2 Device fabrication
ITO glass (CSG Holding Co., Ltd., $24\Omega{\cdot}\mathrm{sq}^{-1},$ ) substrates were cleaned with detergent, deionized water, acetone, and isopropyl alcohol in an ultrasonic bath for $20\mathrm{min}$ followed by drying with $\Nu_{2}$ . After treating the substrate with UV-ozone for $10\mathrm{min},$ , a $30\mathrm{nm}$ thick layer of PEDOT:PSS (Al 4083, H. C. Starck) was spin coated on it. The $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer was spin-coated from the precursor solution having different concentrations with a speed of $5,000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{{s}}$ . The resulting layers were subsequently annealed at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ in a glove box.
The $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ precursor solution was prepared by mixing $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{I}$ with $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ at a molar ratio of 1:1 in anhydrous $_{\mathrm{N,N}}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) $(43\mathrm{~wt.\%}$ solution) at room temperature, stirring for $12\mathrm{{h}}$ inside a nitrogen-filled glove box with oxygen and moisture levels $\leq1\:\mathrm{ppm}$ . Perovskite films were deposited by spin-coating the $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ precursor at $5,000\mathrm{rpm}$ for $30\mathrm{~s~}$ on a HTL-coated ITO glass substrate. Chlorobenzene (CBZ) solution $(200~\upmu\mathrm{L})$ was added to $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ with a delay time of ${\sim}4.5\ s$ after starting the spin-coating process. The obtained films were then dried at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $10\mathrm{min}$ . $C_{60}$ 1 $\cdot40\mathrm{nm})$ , 2,9-dimethyl$^{4,7}$ -diphenyl-1,10-phenanthroline (BCP) $({\sim}8\mathrm{nm})$ , and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ $\scriptstyle\left(\sim100\ n m\right)$ were thermally deposited on the substrate inside the vacuum chamber. The active area of the device is $0.100\mathrm{cm}^{2}$ .
## 2.3 Characterizations
All the photovoltaic measurements were performed in a glove box. The current–voltage curves of the PSCs were recorded using a Keithley 4200 semiconductor characterization system, illuminated by an Oriel 300 W solar simulator (Thermo Oriel 91160-1000) with a AM $1.5\mathrm{G}$ filter at a calibrated intensity of $100\mathrm{m}\mathrm{W}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ , which was determined by a standard silicon reference cell. Both forward and reverse scan modes were employed, with the forward scan ranging from $-0.2$ to
$1.2\mathrm{V}$ and the reverse scan ranging from 1.2 to $-0.2\mathrm{V}$ with the scan rate varying from 0.1 to $0.4\mathrm{V}{\cdot}\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}^{-1}$ .
X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on a SHIMADZU XRD-7000 X-RAY diffractometer with a monochromated $\operatorname{Cu}\mathrm{K}\alpha$ irradiation $(\lambda=1.5418\mathrm{~\AA~})$ ) at a scan rate of $4\mathrm{^{\circ}{\cdot}\mathrm{min^{-1}}}$ . The transmittance of different HTLs was measured using a UV-3100 spectrophotometer (Shimadzu). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) and AFM analyses were performed using Hitachi S-4800 and SPI3800/SPA400 SPM (Seiko Instrument Inc.). XPS measurements were performed using an AXIS Ultra instrument (Kratos UK) at a base pressure of ${\sim}10^{-8}$ torr and $295\mathrm{K}$ . The WF of PEDOT:PSS and $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ thin films were measured using UPS (Riken Keiki). Steady-state photoluminescence (PL) measurements were conducted using an Edinburgh FLS980 fluorescence spectrophotometer with an excitation at $470\mathrm{nm}$ . The excitation light reaches the sample from the substrate side with an incidental angle of $30^{\circ}.$ , and the PL emission is collected from the substrate side. Time-resolved photoluminescence was acquired using the time-correlated single-photon counting technique, and the excitation from a pico-second pulse lamp at the wavelength of $470\mathrm{nm}$ with a repetition frequency of $\sim1{-}20\mathrm{MHz}$ was employed. EIS measurements were performed on a CHI660C electrochemical workstation (Shanghai, China) with $5\mathrm{mV}$ alternating current (AC) amplitude at a frequency range of $100\mathrm{kHz}$ to $1\mathrm{Hz}$ . For performing transient photovoltage/photocurrent decay measurements, a white light bias was generated from an array of diodes (Molex 180081-4320) to simulate 1 sun illumination condition. An Opolette 355 tunable laser system was used as the perturbation source, with an excitation wavelength of $590\mathrm{nm},$ , a pulse width of $7\mathrm{ns,}$ and a repetition frequency of $20\mathrm{Hz}$ . The perturbation light intensity was attenuated to keep the amplitude of transient $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ $(\Delta V_{\mathrm{oc}})$ below $5\mathrm{mV}$ so that $\Delta V_{\mathrm{oc}}\ll V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ . The voltage and current dynamics were recorded on a digital oscilloscope, and the voltages at open circuit and currents at short circuit conditions were measured over a $1\mathrm{M}\Omega$ and a $50\Omega$ resistor, respectively.
## 3 Results and discussion
The WF of transport materials could affect the quasi-Fermi energy splitting of a p–n junction, thereby affecting the output $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of a solar cell.
The difference between the HOMO of HTL and the lowest unoccupied molecule orbital (LUMO) of ETL is reported to determine the upper limit of $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ [30]. UPS analysis was carried out to explore the WFs of both PEDOT:PSS and $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ films. Table 1 lists the WFs of the ITO glass, PEDOT:PSS film, $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ film and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ film measured using UPS, and the detailed results are given in Fig. S2 in the ESM. The WF of $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ is $5.4\mathrm{eV}$ , $0.3\mathrm{eV}$ lower than that of PEDOT:PSS (WF of $5.1\mathrm{eV}\mathrm{\cdot}$ ), which is in agreement with the reported values [31, 32]. The WF of modified $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ $(5.4\mathrm{eV})$ is comparable with the WF of PTAA $(\sim5.25\mathrm{eV})$ [33] and spiro-OMeTAD $({\sim}5.22\ \mathrm{eV})$ [6], commonly used materials in $\mathsf{n{-}i{-}p}$ PSCs. As a reference, the perovskite materials based champion cell is frequently obtained in the $\mathsf{n{-}i{-}p}$ structure, which lines well with the high WF of hole transport materials, e.g., PTAA or spiro-OMeTAD. Theoretically, the inverted perovskite devices based on $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ exhibit a great potential for achieving enhanced $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and PCE. The surface properties of the HTL were investigated using AFM. AFM images of the bare ITO, ITO/ PEDOT:PSS, and $\mathrm{ITO/PEDOT{:}P S S/V_{2}O_{\boldsymbol{x}}}$ are illustrated in Fig. 2, indicating root mean square (r.m.s.) roughness values of 6.99, 2.25, and $2.92\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The $\mathrm{PEDOT:PSS/V_{2}O_{\boldsymbol{x}}}$ bilayer HTL enables the formation of more uniform and smoother surfaces as compared with ITO, whereas the introduction of $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ on PEDOT:PSS does not change the surface properties of pristine PEDOT:PSS. We also investigated the bare $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ surfaces on ITO using AFM to obtain an r.m.s. roughness of $4.51\mathrm{nm}$ . The relatively inferior surface roughness of the bare $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ indicates the beneficial use of PEDOT:PSS $\mathrm{\Delta}/\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ bilayer as the HTL. A smooth and compact HTL is a prerequisite for implementing as an effective buffer layer in photovoltaic devices, as local shorts and current leakages can be avoided. XRD patterns of perovskite films deposited on PEDOT:PSScoated and bilayer HTL coated ITO substrates are shown in Fig. S3 in the ESM. The diffraction peaks corresponding to $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ are observed. Both the perovskite films exhibit a strong diffraction peak at $14.69^{\circ}$ as reported earlier [7], corresponding to a tetragonal structure. The diffraction intensity of the perovskite film deposited on PEDOT:PSS is much higher than that on ${\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{x},$ indicating the influence of surface properties of HTL on the crystallinity of perovskite materials. SEM images of the perovskite films on PEDOT:PSS and $\mathrm{PEDOT:PSS/V_{2}O_{\boldsymbol{x}}}$ are shown in Fig. S4 in the ESM. No observable pinholes are found in both the films. The perovskite film coated on PEDOT:PSS exhibits larger grain sizes (over $500\mathrm{nm}\rangle$ than that on the $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ film $(100-200\mathrm{nm})$ . The differences in the crystal size indicates that the surface of the underneath HTL affects the subsequent nucleation and growth of perovskite materials. Generally, the number or density of nucleation sites largely determines the grain size of the resulting film, and the film with low number of nucleation sites might promote the formation of larger grains. In contrast, the film with smaller grains is obtained when the density of the nucleation site increases. In the present approach, the perovskite materials grown on the inorganic interfacial $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ layer exhibit smaller grains as compared with that on PEDOT:PSS, indicating a higher nucleation center density. This could be attributed to the changes in the surface energy achieved with the introduction of $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ and the perovskite crystal growth occurring preferably via the Frank–van der Merwe (FM) growth mode, where adatoms are preferentially attached to the surface sites resulting in atomically smooth, fully formed layers. The atomically smooth and uniform layers can be observed in the AFM images, as shown in Fig. 3. The surface roughness values of the perovskite film on the bilayer HTL and PEDOT:PSS are ${\sim}4.28$ and ${\sim}5.42\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively. The reduction in the surface roughness could be advantageous for the device characteristics. The inverted planar heterojunction (PHJ) $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ solar cell was subsequently fabricated with the bilayer $\mathrm{PEDOT:PSS/V_{2}O_{\boldsymbol{x}}}$ as the HTL. The device configuration, cross-section SEM image, and the energy diagrams of the prepared device are shown in Fig. 4. We chose $\mathrm{C_{60}/B C P}$ as the ETL. The device is fabricated with an ITO substrate $(200\mathrm{nm})$ , PEDOT:PSS film $(\sim30\mathrm{nm})$ , $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer $(<10\mathrm{nm})$ , active layer $(\sim300\mathrm{nm})$ , $C_{60}$ ${\cdot}40\mathrm{nm})$ , BCP $(\sim8\mathrm{{nm})}$ , and $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}$ electrode $(\sim100\mathrm{nm})$ from bottom to the top. The WFs of PEDOT:PSS and $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ were examined using UPS, and other related values from previous studies are also given [13]. With $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ modification, the WF of HTL undergoes a downward shift, which is essential for enhancing quasi-Fermi level splitting.
Devices based on various HTLs were fabricated using similar fabrication processes to explore the influence of HTL on the performance of solar cells. Different types of HTLs, namely, a pristine PEDOT:PSS layer $(\sim30\mathrm{nm})$ , a thin $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ layer $(<10\mathrm{nm})$ , and a bilayer $\mathrm{PEDOT:PSS/V_{2}O_{\boldsymbol{x}}}$ structure, were used. Table 2 summarizes the current–voltage characteristics of the obtained devices. Series resistance $(R_{s})$ and shunt resistance $(R_{\mathrm{sh}})$ were estimated with the best cell in each batch. The pristine PEDOT:PSS based reference device exhibits a short-circuit current $(J_{\mathrm{sc}}),\ V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ fill factor (FF), and PCE of $22.5\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ , $0.85\mathrm{V},$ , $74.4\%$ , and $14.2\%$ , respectively. On replacing PEDOT:PSS with ${\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{x},$ a higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $0.98\mathrm{V},$ decreased $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $21.9\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ and FF of $68.8\%,$ and slightly enhanced PCE of $14.8\%$ are obtained. The higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ could be possibly ascribed to the higher WF of the $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x},$ leading to a potentially larger quasi-Fermi level splitting and a higher $V_{\mathrm{oc}}.$ . The relatively lower values of $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and FF could be probably attributed to the lower conductivity of $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ than that of PEDOT:PSS [34, 35], which might affect the charge transfer/transport processes in the device. The unsatisfied coverage of the $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ film that leads to a rather low shunt resistance of the device might also affect the FF. The device performance was greatly improved when using $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ and PEDOT:PSS as the bilayer in HTL. Initially, $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ is increased from ${\sim}0.84$ to $1.05\mathrm{V},$ indicating that the $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer modifies the Fermi energy of pristine PEDOT:PSS ${\mathrm{HTL}},$ resulting in an improved band alignment. The short-circuit current density decreases by ${\sim}10\%,$ which could be associated with the lower conductivity of ${\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{x},$ as mentioned above.
To investigate the mechanism of the reduction in $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ further, $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ films with different thicknesses were fabricated on PEDOT:PSS. The thickness of the $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ film was controlled by adjusting the precursor concentration. Transmittance spectra of different HTLs were measured using a UV–vis spectrophotometer. Figure S5 in the ESM presents the transmittance spectra of $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ films of different thicknesses deposited on PEDOT:PSS film coated quartz glass. Nearly identical transmittance values are obtained for all the films. The transmittance of all the samples is over $90\%$ in the range of $350{\mathrm{-}}800{\mathrm{nm}},$ , suggesting that the introduction of the thin $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer has negligible effects on the optical field distribution of the devices as long as the device geometry is unchanged. $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer exhibits a bandgap of ${\sim}2.8\mathrm{eV}$ and no absorption loss in the visible region. However, the performance of the devices significantly changes as different HTLs are employed. Table S1 in the ESM summarizes the current density–voltage $(J{-}V)$ parameters of devices based on HTLs with $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ films having different thicknesses. The thickness of $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ dramatically influences the performance of the solar cells, especially the current density and FF. According to the previously reported results, the conductivity of PEDOT:PSS ranges $\sim1{-}2,000\mathsf{S}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ [33], a few order of magnitude higher than that of ${\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{x},$ which is ${\sim}10^{-3}$ $\mathsf{S}{\cdot}\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ in bulk crystals and even lower in thin films [35]. $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ exhibiting a low conductivity might slow down the charge transport, resulting in a relatively higher resistance for devices with thick $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayers. Thus, the $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer should be as thin as possible to avoid the undesired effect mentioned above, since the layer with a low thickness might induce a full surface coverage to maximize the output $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and FF.
The optimized device fabricated with $0.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{\cdotmL}^{-1}$ $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ precursor solution exhibits $J_{\mathrm{sc}},\ V_{\mathrm{oc}},$ FF, and PCE of $21.2\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2},$ , 1.05 V, $78.5\%,$ , and $17.5\%$ , respectively, under reverse scan mode. The $J{-}V$ curves are shown in Fig. 5(a). The inset in the figure shows the incident photon-to-current efficiency (IPCE) spectrum of the optimized device, with a peak value of $>80\%$ obtained at $550\mathrm{nm}$ . An integrated $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ (from IPCE) of up to $18.5\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is obtained, in agreement with the reported values for other inverted structured devices [36]. We performed $J{-}V$ measurements with the optimized device by changing the scanning directions and the scanning rates, as $J{-}V$ hysteresis would affect the accurate characterization of the device efficiency. Figure 5(a) shows the device characteristics measured by a forward scan, indicating $J_{\mathrm{sc}},$ $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ FF, and PCE of $21.1\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ , $1.05\mathrm{V},$ $75.6\%$ , and $17.0\%$ , respectively. Figure S6 in the ESM shows $J{-}V$ curves recorded with different scanning rates. No obvious photocurrent hysteresis is observed with different scanning directions or scanning rates. To verify the measured efficiency, we examined the steady-state photocurrent output at the maximum power point, as the steady state output is the most accepted parameter to evaluate the actual device performance. Below $0.87\mathrm{V},$ the photocurrent reaches its maximum value within a few seconds, and a photocurrent output of $\mathrm{\sim}19.7\mathrm{mA}{\cdot}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ is obtained, as indicated in Fig. 5(b). A stabilized efficiency of ${\sim}17.1\%$ is achieved, as comparable to that measured from the $J{-}V$ curve $(17.5\%)$ , indicating the fabrication of a new type of solar cells exhibiting nearly free hysteresis.
EIS measurements were performed to investigate the interfacial charge transfer process in devices w/w-o $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ layers. Figure 6(a) presents the Nyquist plots of the two devices measured in dark conditions, by setting the bias voltages for the PEDOT:PSS-based device and the bilayer HTL-based device at 0.57 and
$0.7\mathrm{V},$ respectively. The equivalent circuit to fit the experimental data is shown in the inset of Fig. 6(a), which consists of two parallel resistor-capacitor circuit (RC) elements, with a series resistance of $R$ . The left side corresponds to the high frequency region related to the hole transport process in the hole transport material (HTM), whereas the right side corresponds to the low frequency region associated with the chemical capacitance $(C_{\upmu})$ of the system. Recombination processes occur in both the devices, and the corresponding rate is inversely proportional to $R_{\mathrm{rec}}.R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ obtained from the fittings at different bias voltages are presented in Fig. 6(b). $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ of the cells decreases with an increase in the bias voltage, similar to previously reported results. According to Perez et al., $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ at a high applied voltage would significantly affect the $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ [37], which could explain the higher $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ and higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ obtained with the bilayer HTL-based device. In contrast, the low $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ values obtained at low applied voltages are reported to adversely affect the FF since such systems behave like cells exhibiting small shunt resistances [37]. At a low applied bias voltage, the bilayer structured HTL-based device exhibits higher $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ and FF (Fig. 6(b)). Regardless of the applied voltage, the calculated $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ of the PEDOT:PSS device is one order of magnitude lower than that of the bilayer structured HTL-based device, implying a faster recombination rate for the PEDOT:PSS based device than that based on bilayer HTL. The decreased charge carrier recombination in the $\mathrm{PEDOT:PSS/V_{2}O_{\boldsymbol{x}}}$ based devices leads to an improvement in the $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ and FF, confirming that
PEDOT:PSS/ $\mathrm{\DeltaV}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ is suitable as the HTL in PSCs.
PL and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) measurements were carried out on $\mathrm{ITO/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}},$ ITO/PEDOT:PSS $\mathrm{{'CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}},$ and ITO/PEDOT:PSS/ $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}/\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ to examine the radiative and nonradiative recombination channels in the materials as well as the charge transfer between the absorber and transport materials (Fig. 7). A strong PL quenching of the emission indicates evidence for the efficient charge transfer from the photoactive layer to the charge transport layer. The quenching of the PL emission by the PEDOT:PSS and $\mathrm{PEDOT:PSS/V_{2}O_{\boldsymbol{x}}}$ layers can be clearly observed in Fig. 7(a). TRPL measurements were employed further to investigate the radiative and nonradiative carrier recombination of films. As indicated in Fig. 7(b), a dramatic decrease of the lifetime is achieved with the ITO/perovskite in ITO/ PEDOT:PSS $\mathrm{{'CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}}$ and in $\mathrm{ITO/PEDOT{:}P S S/V_{2}O_{\boldsymbol{x}}/}$ $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ films. The lifetime of carriers and/or excitons in the corresponding films is obtained by fitting the results with two exponential decay curves, and the long lifetime is used for comparison. The lifetime values of ITO/PEDOT:PSS $\mathrm{{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}}$ and ITO/PEDOT:PSS $\mathrm{\langleN_{2}O_{\it x}/C H_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ film are ${\sim}10.0$ and $10.5\mathrm{ns},$ respectively, whereas that of the reference sample is ${\sim}75\mathrm{ns,}$ , indicating an effective charge carrier extraction within the perovskite/HTL interface. An efficient charge carrier transfer occurs from the absorber to the transport materials, alleviating the charge accumulation in the interfaces, which might contribute to the absence of hysteresis in the PEDOT:PSS $\mathrm{{V}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{x}$
based PSCs.
To examine the origin of the improved device characteristics, we employed transient photocurrent and photovoltage measurements for investigating the carrier dynamics along the entire pathway in the completed cells. Figure 8(a) shows the results of transient photocurrent measurements for the PEDOT:PSS-based device and the bilayer structured HTL-based device. The bilayer structured HTL-based device exhibits a comparable photocurrent decay with that of the reference device in accordance with the PL results, where perovskites with both the HTL materials exhibit a strong quenching effect. However, the comparable photocurrent decay time cannot explain the discrepancy obtained in the short circuit current in both the devices (Table 2). Typically, a higher $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ is accompanied with a more efficient charge transfer in the devices. In this case, we ascribe the comparable photocurrent decay in both the devices to the high WF of the $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer, which presumably induces a stronger built-in field in the p–n junction to overcome the barrier between the perovskite layer and HTL, thereby accelerating the charge transport.
Transient photovoltage measurements, which correlate to the electron lifetime in the absorber, provide insights about the carrier recombination rates in the cell. Based on the voltage decay time, the device with the bilayer HTL is found to exhibit ${\sim}2\cdot$ -fold longer electron lifetime than the reference cell $(1.4~\upmu s$ versus $0.7\upmu\mathrm{s})$ , which could be attributed to the slower interfacial charge recombination. The rapid charge transport accelerated by the stronger built-in field in the PN junction prevents the charge accumulation at the perovskite/charge extraction layer interface. The obtained results are in good agreement with the EIS results in which the device with the $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ interlayer demonstrates a much higher recombination resistance than that with the pristine PEDOT:PSS as the HTL.
## 4 Conclusions
In this study, an inorganic interfacial material, ${\mathrm{V}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{x},$ was combined with PEDOT:PSS to form a bilayer structured HTL that was used to fabricate PSCs with an inverted planner heterojunction structure. Compared with the reference PEDOT:PSS devices, the new device exhibited a higher $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ of $1.05\mathrm{V},$ associated with its higher WF. A PCE of $17.5\%$ with nearly free hysteresis and a stabilized efficiency of $17.1\%$ were achieved. EIS measurements reveal that the bilayer HTL based device exhibited a higher recombination resistance than the PEDOT:PSS based device, leading to an increase in the output $V_{\mathrm{{oc}}}$ . Transient photocurrent decay and photovoltage decay measurements indicate that a comparable charge transport process and a much slower carrier recombination were obtained with the bilayer HTL as compared with the reference device. Our study demonstrates that the novel bilayer material acts as an excellent candidate as the hole extraction layer in inverted perovskite devices, since $\mathrm{V}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{x}$ affects the interface properties, including surface energy as well as optical and electrical properties. By using inorganic materials as the interfacial materials, an enhanced stability can be achieved, which cannot be readily achieved with organic interfacial materials. The experimental results and the fundamental insights could be useful in the development of highly efficient single junction or multi-junction solar cells and other optoelectronic devices.
